{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MsFTDMuJPagwDKfEZvEAGX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Summary\n2\t\n3\tRestored the frosted-glass (tinted + blurred) surface on the two \"expandable\n4\toverlay search input\" components — the library search (shared across all 8\n5\tlibrary routes) and the contacts search — so they match the reference\n6\ttreatment used by `UiNotifications`. Verification uncovered that the\n7\tTailwind class change alone was not enough: a pre-existing global CSS rule\n8\twas silently forcing the inputs back to opaque, so the fix required a small\n9\taddition (`!important`) beyond the originally-scoped plain class swap.\n10\t\n11\t## Changes\n12\t\n13\tTASK-1: Restyled `src/lib/components/libraries/shared/Search.svelte` — replaced\n14\t`bg-transparent ... backdrop-blur-xs` with the same tinted/blurred treatment\n15\tas `UiNotifications` (`bg-c-neutral/60 ... backdrop-blur-sm ... dark:bg-s-dark/80`).\n16\t\n17\tTASK-2: Restyled `src/lib/components/contacts/Search.svelte` — replaced the\n18\tfully opaque `bg-c-bg-surface` (no blur) with the same `bg-c-neutral/60 ...\n19\tbackdrop-blur-sm ... dark:bg-s-dark/80` treatment.\n20\t\n21\tTASK-3: Visual verification (light + dark, over varied backgrounds) via a\n22\ttemporary preview route + `shot`/Playwright, screenshots committed under\n23\t`screenshots/`. This verification found that the TASK-1/TASK-2 class changes\n24\trendered as fully opaque in the browser despite being correct Tailwind\n25\tclasses. Root cause: `src/styles/components/forms.css` sets an *unlayered*\n26\t`background-color` on all non-checkbox/radio/file inputs (added previously\n27\tto theme `@tailwindcss/forms`'s white default across the app). Per CSS\n28\tCascade Layers, an unlayered rule always wins over a `@layer`'d rule\n29\t(Tailwind utilities live in a named `utilities` layer) regardless of source\n30\torder or specificity — so the background utility was being silently\n31\toverridden only on `<input>` elements (confirmed with `div` vs `input`\n32\tcomparison and Chrome DevTools' `CSS.getMatchedStylesForNode`). Fixed by\n33\tmarking just the background utilities on these two inputs `!important`\n34\t(`!bg-c-neutral/60 ... dark:!bg-s-dark/80`), which is enough to beat the\n35\tglobal rule without touching `forms.css` (used by every other input in the\n36\tapp, out of scope to change). Re-verified after the fix: both inputs now show\n37\tthe backdrop blur correctly bleeding/tinting over a colorful gradient and a\n38\tstriped background, in both light and dark mode, with typed text remaining\n39\tlegible in light mode.\n40\t\n41\t## Known issues / follow-ups\n42\t\n43\t- While verifying dark mode, both search inputs (and, for comparison, the\n44\t  reference `UiNotifications` component itself) render body text in a dark\n45\t  color with no explicit `dark:text-*` class, giving low contrast against\n46\t  the darkened translucent surface. This is a pre-existing, app-wide\n47\t  characteristic (confirmed identical on the reference component), not\n48\t  something introduced or worsened by this fix, and out of scope for a\n49\t  \"restore transparency/blur\" task — flagging it here in case it's worth a\n50\t  separate follow-up.\n51\t- As noted by the analyst, the two Search.svelte files remain near-duplicate\n52\t  implementations; not consolidated here per \"don't refactor unrelated code\".\n53\t- A temporary preview route and `npm ci`-restored `node_modules` were used\n54\t  for verification only; the preview route was removed and is not part of\n55\t  this branch's history (node_modules stays gitignored as before).\n56\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d598b0f1-fa8c-42e2-a89e-0964d60f996c","uuid":"1cbc1965-5bfe-4f78-9cdf-79ea19182aae","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:47:53.326Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/pair_transparency-in-search/implementation.md","content":"## Summary\n\nRestored the frosted-glass (tinted + blurred) surface on the two \"expandable\noverlay search input\" components — the library search (shared across all 8\nlibrary routes) and the contacts search — so they match the reference\ntreatment used by `UiNotifications`. Verification uncovered that the\nTailwind class change alone was not enough: a pre-existing global CSS rule\nwas silently forcing the inputs back to opaque, so the fix required a small\naddition (`!important`) beyond the originally-scoped plain class swap.\n\n## Changes\n\nTASK-1: Restyled `src/lib/components/libraries/shared/Search.svelte` — replaced\n`bg-transparent ... backdrop-blur-xs` with the same tinted/blurred treatment\nas `UiNotifications` (`bg-c-neutral/60 ... backdrop-blur-sm ... dark:bg-s-dark/80`).\n\nTASK-2: Restyled `src/lib/components/contacts/Search.svelte` — replaced the\nfully opaque `bg-c-bg-surface` (no blur) with the same `bg-c-neutral/60 ...\nbackdrop-blur-sm ... dark:bg-s-dark/80` treatment.\n\nTASK-3: Visual verification (light + dark, over varied backgrounds) via a\ntemporary preview route + `shot`/Playwright, screenshots committed under\n`screenshots/`. This verification found that the TASK-1/TASK-2 class changes\nrendered as fully opaque in the browser despite being correct Tailwind\nclasses. Root cause: `src/styles/components/forms.css` sets an *unlayered*\n`background-color` on all non-checkbox/radio/file inputs (added previously\nto theme `@tailwindcss/forms`'s white default across the app). Per CSS\nCascade Layers, an unlayered rule always wins over a `@layer`'d rule\n(Tailwind utilities live in a named `utilities` layer) regardless of source\norder or specificity — so the background utility was being silently\noverridden only on `<input>` elements (confirmed with `div` vs `input`\ncomparison and Chrome DevTools' `CSS.getMatchedStylesForNode`). Fixed by\nmarking just the background utilities on these two inputs `!important`\n(`!bg-c-neutral/60 ... dark:!bg-s-dark/80`), which is enough to beat the\nglobal rule without touching `forms.css` (used by every other input in the\napp, out of scope to change). Re-verified after the fix: both inputs now show\nthe backdrop blur correctly bleeding/tinting over a colorful gradient and a\nstriped background, in both light and dark mode, with typed text remaining\nlegible in light mode.\n\n## Known issues / follow-ups\n\n- While verifying dark mode, both search inputs (and, for comparison, the\n  reference `UiNotifications` component itself) render body text in a dark\n  color with no explicit `dark:text-*` class, giving low contrast against\n  the darkened translucent surface. This is a pre-existing, app-wide\n  characteristic (confirmed identical on the reference component), not\n  something introduced or worsened by this fix, and out of scope for a\n  \"restore transparency/blur\" task — flagging it here in case it's worth a\n  separate follow-up.\n- As noted by the analyst, the two Search.svelte files remain near-duplicate\n  implementations; not consolidated here per \"don't refactor unrelated code\".\n- A temporary preview route and `npm ci`-restored `node_modules` were used\n  for verification only; the preview route was removed and is not part of\n  this branch's history (node_modules stays gitignored as before).\n","numLines":56,"startLine":1,"totalLines":56}}}
